upstream sha256
85883bb4b626fec08984e69d40945bf62aad74f0ca5aa35127b4f8c0f3ab1f31  connector_banjo_tooie_bizhawk.lua

256,935 bytes, 9,188 lines. Its header credits Mike Jackson (jjjj12212) with help from Rose
(Oktorose) and the OoT Archipelago team -- the same lineage as connectors/oot.

Where this one came from needs saying, because it is not where the others came from.

AP_Banjo-Tooie is the world's "PR version" repository, a fork of Archipelago, and this Lua
is NOT shipped in the v4.13.1 release of jjjj12212/Archipelago-BanjoTooie. That release
ships banjo_tooie.apworld, and the only Lua inside it is assets/BT_companion.lua -- twenty
lines that call client.saveram() on a map change and nothing else. Upstream's current
emulator path does not use a connector script at all: banjo_tooie/emu_loader attaches to
the emulator's process and reads its RDRAM directly, and banjo_tooie/client/game.py holds
the game logic that this Lua holds.

So why fork a script upstream has moved away from: the protocol it speaks is still
maintained. BTClient.py still carries the socket transport on port 21221, still gates it on
`script_version = 5`, and that is the path their EverDrive connector uses for real
hardware. This Lua reports SCRIPT_VERSION = 5, so it matches the released client exactly.
Forking it gives AP64 the same relationship to Banjo-Tooie that connectors/oot has to
OoT -- a script speaking a client's own protocol -- without reimplementing 562 locations
of game logic in Rust.

The risk that comes with that: upstream maintains the protocol for their EverDrive
connector, not for this Lua, so the Lua can drift from the client in a way connectors/oot
cannot. If SCRIPT_VERSION stops matching the client's script_version,
the client refuses the connection and says so, which is at least a loud failure.

AP64 delta: BT_VERSION, "4.11.6" upstream, is "4.13.1" here.

That drift is the SILENT one, and it is the reason to watch this file. SCRIPT_VERSION is
checked by the client, which says so; BT_VERSION is checked by the CONNECTOR, in
process_slot(), against the client's slot_version -- and a mismatch latches VERROR and
answers every later poll with a bare keep-alive, forever. The client shows nothing but
"will be sent when Banjo-Tooie is loaded", the socket stays up, no check is ever sent and
no item is ever delivered. It cost an evening on a console before a dump of real game
memory reproduced it offline in three polls.

The value came from that dump: the ROM's own getRomVersion() reports 4.13.1 and the client
reports 4.13.1, so 4.13.1 is what both ends already agree on. Verified compatible: every
one of the 1,106 location IDs this Lua can report is defined by the 4.13.1 apworld, so it
can never send an unknown check. The 32 IDs 4.13.1 defines that this Lua never reports
(1230960-1230991) are Mumbo Token goal locations that BTClient.py derives for itself in
mumbo_tokens_loc(), from locations this Lua does report -- not a gap.

Since the released apworld ships no connector Lua at all, no upstream release will ever
update this string. tests/bt.rs pins it, and models 4.13.1 on both sides so a drift fails
there rather than on a console.

Their EverDrive connector is a separate prebuilt binary (banjo_tooie_everdrive_connector.exe,
4.4 MB) whose source is in neither repository, and it supports the EverDrive 3.0 and X7 --
their own setup guide says the EverDrive-64 PRO may not be compatible, and it does not
mention the SummerCart64. So AP64's contribution here is SC64 support, not hardware support
in general.

process_slot() is an INITIALISATION channel, not just a protocol handshake.

This is the thing about this world that is not visible from the protocol, and it is what
made the BT_VERSION mismatch above cost a day instead of a minute. Banjo-Tooie's randomizer
does not ship its settings inside the ROM. The client sends them, and process_slot() writes
them into the BTHACK's settings block with 39 setSetting* calls. The client's own help text
is blunt about the dependency: "the ROM refuses to boot until settings are populated".

Every one of those calls is AFTER the version check, so a mismatch returns early and writes
none of them. The result is not a quiet connector -- it is a ROM that has never been fully
built, running on settings-shaped memory the boot DMA happened to leave behind. Egg
Behaviour is one of those settings, which is why cycling eggs with R froze a console.

The lesson for any connector added here: if a world's connector initialises the game, then
a connector that answers politely while initialising nothing is WORSE than one that refuses
outright, because the game keeps running and misbehaves somewhere unrelated. Look for an
early return between a validation failure and the game-state writes -- and prefer failing
loudly over answering a keep-alive forever. None of this leaves a trace in RAM maps, hook
rates or bus traffic, so measuring those first is measuring the wrong thing.

